\description{
Follows each organization across years and counts, for every market and
year \code{t}, the organizations that entered (present in \code{t}, not in \code{t - 1})
and exited (present in \code{t - 1}, then absent for at least \code{absent_years}
years). Rates use the stock in \code{t - 1} as the denominator, with the
market and subsector the organization had in \code{t - 1} for exits and in \code{t}
for entries. Organizations that move between markets count as neither.
}
\section{What an exit means}{

In the filers universe an exit is an \strong{exit from filing}: an
organization that stops filing a Form 990 or 990-EZ may have dropped
below the filing threshold and switched to the 990-N postcard, so an exit
is not a death. In the registered universe an exit is removal from the
IRS registry: revocation, termination or merger.
}

\section{Gaps and censoring}{

Absences shorter than \code{absent_years} between two appearances are filled:
the organization counts as present, so a late or missed filing is neither
an exit nor a re-entry. A longer absence ends with a re-entry. Exits in
the last \code{absent_years - 1} observed years cannot be confirmed yet and are
reported as \code{NA}, as are all rates for the first observed year, which has
no prior year.
}

\section{Mass revocations of 2010-2012}{

The Pension Protection Act revoked the exemptions of hundreds of
thousands of organizations that had not filed for three years, almost all
of them in 2010-2012. \code{EXITS_PPA} counts exits in those years that
coincide with a revocation. \code{drop_ppa_revocations = TRUE} leaves them out
of \code{EXITS} and the rates.
}

